    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d43">

                <pb facs="0054" n="34" sameAs="frus:frus1864p3/pg34"/>

                <head>Mr. Seward to Mr.
                        Dayton</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 473.]</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Department of State,</hi>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Washington,</hi></placeName>
                        <date calendar="gregorian" when="1864-02-08"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >February</hi> 8, 1864.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Your despatch of the 22d ultimo, No. 404,
                    has been received.</p>
                <p>You will have learned from instructions which are on their way to you that the
                    President was not satisfied with the course which the French government had
                    thought proper to pursue with reference to the Florida, Georgia, and
                    Rappahannock. It appears, however, from your note to Mr. Drouyn de l’Huys of <pb
                        facs="0055" n="35" xml:id="pg35"/> the 22d ultimo, that you had anticipated,
                    in respect to the Florida at least, the instructions adverted to. Our own law,
                    as you are aware, forbids not only the fitting out and arming in our ports of
                    vessels to make war on a country with which we may be at peace, but also
                    prohibits any augmentation of the armament of a foreign belligerent in a United
                    States port. We have a right to expect the same prohibition in French ports
                    against insurgent vessels. There must necessarily be some anxiety here upon this
                    subject until the determination of the French government can be known.</p>
                <p>I am, sir, your obedient servant,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>WILLIAM H. SEWARD.</signed></closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">William L. Dayton</hi> Esq., <hi
                            rendition="#italic">&amp;c., &amp;c., &amp;c.</hi></p>
                </postscript>
            </div>
